WEBVTT

00:00:03.070 --> 00:00:06.340
Three tricky moments in the values conversation.

00:00:06.390 --> 00:00:10.580
Moment one: a post-it is clearly about one person.

00:00:10.630 --> 00:00:14.560
Everyone knows who “interrupts others in meetings” is about.

00:00:14.610 --> 00:00:17.839
Don’t name the person, and don’t remove the post-it.

00:00:17.889 --> 00:00:22.400
Keep the focus on the behaviour: “This is something we all do sometimes.

00:00:22.450 --> 00:00:24.580
Do we agree it belongs in OUT?”

00:00:24.630 --> 00:00:29.220
If needed, follow up with the person privately afterwards, with care.

00:00:29.270 --> 00:00:32.660
Moment two: the contract is too polite.

00:00:32.710 --> 00:00:36.720
Everything in it is nice, and nothing in it is a real change.

00:00:36.770 --> 00:00:40.040
That usually means people don’t feel safe enough yet.

00:00:40.090 --> 00:00:45.080
Try asking: “What’s one behaviour that happens here, that we haven’t written down?”

00:00:45.130 --> 00:00:47.080
Then give silent time.

00:00:47.130 --> 00:00:50.880
Or collect post-its anonymously and read them out yourself.

00:00:50.930 --> 00:00:54.580
Moment three: people disagree about a behaviour.

00:00:54.630 --> 00:00:58.700
One person thinks “answering messages after hours” is dedication.

00:00:58.750 --> 00:01:01.240
Another thinks it’s a problem. Great.

00:01:01.290 --> 00:01:04.480
That’s a real conversation about how you want to work.

00:01:04.530 --> 00:01:11.840
Talk it through, and if you can’t agree, write down what you can agree on: “Nobody is expected to answer after six.”

00:01:11.890 --> 00:01:14.360
The hardest part comes after the session.

00:01:14.410 --> 00:01:18.560
A contract only works if people use it. That includes you.

00:01:18.610 --> 00:01:21.500
When you break it yourself, say so out loud.

00:01:21.550 --> 00:01:29.900
And when someone else breaks it, refer to the contract, not your personal opinion: “We agreed to disagree in the meeting, not after it.”

00:01:29.950 --> 00:01:32.880
It turns criticism into a shared standard.

00:01:32.930 --> 00:01:38.464
Look at the contract again every few months, or when someone joins. Is it still true? What’s missing?

